315:/// Remove one operand the way `rm` does (UTILITY_VERBS_LOCAL_PARITY
316:/// slice 2, AC-3 and AC-12):
317:///
318:/// - an operand that is not there fails with the system's own error
319:///   (`No such file or directory`), unless `force`;
320:/// - a directory fails with `Is a directory` unless `recursive`, and
321:///   nothing in it is touched;
322:/// - a symbolic link is removed as the link it is and never followed, at
323:///   the operand and below it;
324:/// - a tree is removed contents first, and an entry that cannot be removed
325:///   is REPORTED and passed over — the walk goes on, and every directory
326:///   above the failure is left standing without a second report, as
327:///   `rm -r` leaves it. ([`execute_deletion_plan`] stops at the first
328:///   failure; a mirror's delete pass wants that, a user's `rm` does not.)
329:///
330:/// `as_directory` is how the operand was TYPED (cr-uv1-1): with a
331:/// separator after it. `rm` then removes it only if it is a directory —
332:/// `rm doc/` of a file is `Not a directory`, which `-f` passes over as it
333:/// does a missing operand, the file left where it is. A link typed so is
334:/// not removed and NOT GONE THROUGH: `Is a directory` without `-r` when it
335:/// points to one, as `rm` says, and `Not a directory` with it. That last
336:/// is where this is not `rm`, which empties the directory the link points
337:/// to before saying the same words; nothing here is ever removed through
338:/// a link.
339:///
340:/// `target` must lie in `canonical_root`, and that is found out before
341:/// anything is removed. One that does not — or whose place cannot be found
342:/// out, below a directory that cannot be searched — is a FAILURE OF THAT
343:/// OPERAND, like any other (cr-uv1-3): `rm a blocked/b c` removes `a` and
344:/// `c` and says why `blocked/b` stays. `Err` is for what is not about one
345:/// operand: a cancelled operation.
346:///
347:/// # Anchoring (cr-uv1-2)
348:///
349:/// A check made on a path says nothing about what that path names a
350:/// moment later: a directory inside the root can be swapped for a link
351:/// that leaves it. So on Unix nothing here is found by a path twice. The
352:/// operand's directory is reached from the root one name at a time, each
353:/// opened IN the directory before it and never through a link; every
354:/// directory of the tree is opened the same way, IN the one that listed
355:/// it; and a directory is listed, and emptied, through the one descriptor
356:/// it was opened with (`anchored::Dir`). The directories on the way to
357:/// the operand are opened to be searched and no more (`anchored::Passage`,
358:/// cr-uv2-4): `rm` asks nothing else of them. What cannot be opened so — a
359:/// directory that became a link, one that cannot be read — is one
360:/// reported failure, and nothing is removed by its path instead.
361:///
362:/// Elsewhere the standard library's own recursive removal does the tree:
363:/// it is anchored to handles in the same way, but it stops at the first
364:/// entry it cannot remove, so a tree with one such entry is left with
365:/// whatever had not gone yet and one failure, reported for the operand.
366:pub fn remove_as_rm(
367:    target: &Path,
368:    as_directory: bool,
369:    canonical_root: Option<&Path>,
370:    options: RmOptions,
371:    abort: Option<&AtomicBool>,
372:) -> Result<Removal, DeletionError> {
373:    let mut failures = Vec::new();
374:    let called_off = || abort.is_some_and(|abort| abort.load(Ordering::Acquire));
375:    let removed = remove_as_rm_reporting(
376:        target,
377:        as_directory,
378:        canonical_root,
379:        options,
380:        Some(&called_off),
381:        &mut |failure| {
382:            failures.push(failure);
383:            Ok(())
384:        },
385:    )?;
386:    Ok(Removal { removed, failures })
387:}
