46:/// Render a relative path for request fields whose module-root identity is
47:/// `"."` rather than the manifest convention's empty string.
48:pub fn request_path_to_posix(path: &Path) -> String {
49:    let path = relative_path_to_posix(path);
50:    if path.is_empty() {
51:        ".".to_string()
52:    } else {
53:        path
54:    }
55:}
56:
57:/// Render a relative path expressed as a string (e.g. user input or a
58:/// wire-supplied value of unknown provenance) as canonical POSIX form,
59:/// applying the same component-aware semantics as
60:/// [`relative_path_to_posix`]. On Windows, a user-typed `Folder\file` is
61:/// split on the native separator; on POSIX, `Folder\file` is a single
62:/// filename component and is preserved verbatim.
63:///
64:/// **Trailing-separator semantics are preserved** because for user
65:/// input the trailing separator is a meaningful UX signal — "this
66:/// prefix names a directory; complete *inside* it" (the rsync /
67:/// shell-completion convention). `Path::components()` strips trailing
68:/// separators, so we detect them on the raw input and re-attach a
69:/// canonical `/` after the canonicalization. On POSIX a trailing
70:/// backslash is **not** a separator (it's a literal filename byte) and
71:/// stays as part of the last component; on Windows a trailing native
72:/// `\` IS a separator and round-trips as `/`. Round-1 reopen
73:/// (review) — `sub/` was canonicalizing to `sub`, which broke
74:/// `split_completion_prefix`'s "list inside sub/" path.
75:pub fn relative_str_to_posix(s: &str) -> String {
76:    let trailing_sep = s
77:        .chars()
78:        .next_back()
79:        .map(std::path::is_separator)
80:        .unwrap_or(false);
81:    let canonical = relative_path_to_posix(Path::new(s));
82:    if trailing_sep && !canonical.is_empty() && !canonical.ends_with('/') {
83:        let mut out = canonical;
84:        out.push('/');
85:        out
86:    } else {
87:        canonical
88:    }
89:}
