3://!
4://! `blit rm [-r] [-f] TARGET...`, each operand treated as the Unix command
5://! treats it: a directory needs `-r`, an operand that is not there is an
6://! error unless `-f`, the other operands are processed all the same, and
7://! the exit status is 0 only when every operand is gone. What it prints
8://! is `rm`'s: nothing on success, `cannot remove 'OPERAND': REASON` for
9://! each entry that could not be removed — every one, as the daemon
10://! reports it.
11://!
12://! The one place it follows blit and not `rm`: it asks once before
13://! anything is sent, as `mirror` and `move` do, unless `-y` — or `-f`,
14://! which means "never ask" in `rm` itself, so `blit rm -rf` behaves as
15://! `rm -rf` does.
